    "knowledge_point": "Adding Rational Expressions in Multiple Variables With Common Factors in the Denominators",
    "problem": "Simplify $\\frac{5}{2-h}-\\frac{3}{g(2-h)}.$",
    "worked_solution": "In order to subtract the two fractions, we need them to have a common denominator. Here, we can put both fractions over a common denominator of $g(2-h)$ if we multiply the top and bottom of the first fraction by $g:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{2-h}-\\frac{3}{g(2-h)} & = \\\\ \\frac{g}{g}\\cdot \\frac{5}{2-h}-\\frac{3}{g(2-h)} & = \\\\ \\frac{5g}{g(2-h)}-\\frac{3}{g(2-h)} & = \\\\ \\frac{5g-3}{g(2-h)} & \\end{aligned}\n$$",
